30,batch500_0432,test,Cloud Security,LanG 2_0_ Energy-Efficient Agentic AI for Semi-Automated Sec.txt,MODEL_DUZELTME_ADAYI,What is the F1 score achieved by the small finetuned models compared to the classical models in alert classification?,"The small finetuned models statistically match the classical models on alert classification, incident grading, and rule writing for 5.6 to 59 times less energy, while the classical models match them on two of three tasks.","How do small fine-tuned models compare with a frontier model in energy consumption for SOC alert classification, incident grading, and rule writing?","In the cited study, small fine-tuned models statistically match the frontier model on those three tasks while using 5.6 to 59 times less energy.",F1 puanı sorusu yerine pasajdaki açık enerji verimliliği bulgusunu soruyoruz.,"Against Claude Opus 5.5, scoring flows against each network’s normal traffic reaches 73.42% F1 on unseen networks with no false alarm on 100 benign flows, against 58.10% with 49.00% false positives at no less than 650 times the energy per flow, and small finetuned models statistically match it on alert classification, incident grading and rule writing for 5.6 to 59 times less energy, while classical models match them on two of three tasks. The frontier model stays ahead only in naming attack families on a new network, until about 50 analyst labels exist. The safeguards held under prompt injection, label poisoning and ledger tampering where text filters failed, and one workstation analyzed 3.33 million records in 6.4 minutes, over 30 million an hour, without deploying a rule. Within these limits, small local models carry most SOC detection, a frontier model and a few analyst labels help on new networks, and the guarantees come from structural safeguards. With small samples and one workstation, real deployments with beachhead users come next. IBM Security and Ponemon Institute, “Cost of a data breach report 2024,” IBM Corporation, Tech. Rep., 2024. L. Yang, Z. Chen, C. Wang, Z. Zhang, S. Booma, P. Cao, C. Adam, A. Withers, Z. Kalbarczyk, R. K. Iyer et al., “True attacks, attack attempts, or benign triggers? An empirical measurement of network alerts in a security operations center,” in Proc. 33rd USENIX Secur. Symp., 2024, pp. 1525–1542. S. Wu, X. Sun, E. Dong, Z. Wang, C. Zhao, and J. Yang, “Alert2Vec: Eliminating alert fatigue by embedding security alerts through subgraph learning,” IEEE Trans. Dependable Secure Comput., vol. 23, no. 1, pp. S. Freitas and A. Gharib, “GraphWeaver: Billion-scale cybersecurity incident correlation,” in Proc. 33rd ACM Int. Conf. Inf. Knowl. Manage. (CIKM), 2024, pp. 4479–4486. Y. Liu, Z. Xu, Z. Luo, J. Shang, S. Zhang, H. Zhang, and T. Liu, “MuSAR: Multi-step attack reconstruction from lightweight security logs via event-level semantic ",,
32,batch_new500_0356,train,Cryptography,OWASP_Application_Security_Verification_Standard_5.0.0_en.txt,ORIJINAL_PDF_KONTROLU_GEREKLI,What is the minimum version of glibc required for the Linux kernel's special file to provide secure random bytes directly from the kernel's entropy source?,The minimum version of glibc required for the Linux kernel's special file to provide secure random bytes directly from the kernel's entropy source is glibc 2.25+.,,,"Çıkarılan metindeki çok sütunlu tablo glibc sürümü, Linux özel dosyası ve API'yi karıştırmış. Orijinal PDF tablo satırı kontrol edilmeli.","Based stream. Found in iOS SecRandomCopyBytes and Android Secure Random with the correct settings Linux kernel’s special file for providing random data Provides high‑quality, entropy sources from Application Security Verification Standard implementations, such as BCryptGenRandom set by OpenBSD, available in Linux glibc 2.25+ and Provides secure random bytes directly from the kernel’s entropy source with a straightforward and minimal API. It’s more modern and avoids pitfalls associated with older APIs. The underlying hash function used with HMAC‑DRBG or Hash‑DRBG must be approved for this us‑ This section provides additional information for V11.3 Encryption Algorithms. Approved cipher algorithms are listed in order of preference. Symmetric Key Algorithms Salsa 20 specification Extending the Salsa20 nonce Application Security Verification Standard Symmetric Key Algorithms Block ciphers, such as AES, can be used with different modes of operations. Many modes of oper‑ ations, such as Electronic codebook (ECB), are insecure and must not be used. The Galois/Counter Mode (GCM) and Counter with cipher block chaining message authentication code (CCM) modes of operations provide authenticated encryption and should be used in modern applications. Approved modes are listed in order of preference. • All encrypted messages must be authenticated. For ANY use of CBC mode there MUST be an associated hashing MAC algorithm to validate the message. In general, this MUST be applied in the Encrypt‑Then‑Hash method (but TLS 1.2 uses Hash‑Then‑Encrypt instead). If this cannot be guaranteed, then CBC MUST NOT be used. The only application where encryption without a MAC algorithm is allowed is disk encryption. • If CBC is used, it shall be guaranteed that the verification of the padding is performed in con‑ Application Security Verification Standard • When using CCM‑8, the MAC tag only has 64 bits of security. This does not conform to require‑ ment 6.2.9 which requires at least 128 bits of s",,
35,extra_20261108_966,train,Cryptography,Establishing Wireless Robust Security Networks_ A Guide to IEEE 802.11i.txt,MODEL_DUZELTME_ADAYI,What mechanism does CCMP use to ensure that the same key can be used for both confidentiality and integrity?,"CCMP uses a single cryptographic key for both confidentiality and integrity. This key is derived from the combination of the Temporal Key (KeyID) and the 48-bit packet number (PN), allowing the key to serve dual purposes without compromising either aspect.","How does CCMP use a single temporal key for confidentiality and integrity, and what is the role of the packet number?",CCMP uses AES-CCM with one temporal key to encrypt data and protect its integrity. The 48-bit packet number helps construct the nonce; it is not used to derive the temporal key.,KeyID/PN ile anahtar türetildiği iddiası çıkarıldı; PN nonce üretiminde kullanılıyor.,"CCMP key space has size 2128 and uses a 48-bit packet number (PN) to construct a nonce to prevent replay attacks. The construction of the nonce allows the key to be used for both integrity and confidentiality without compromising either.44 As the long-term IEEE 802.11 WLAN solution for confidentiality and integrity, CCMP uses CCM, which was specifically designed to possess the following characteristics: A single cryptographic key for confidentiality and integrity to minimize complexity and maximize performance (minimize key scheduling time)45 Integrity protection of the packet header and packet payload, in addition to providing confidentiality of the payload Computation of some cryptographic parameters prior to the receipt of packets to enable fast comparisons when they arrive, which reduces latency Small footprint (hardware or software implementation size) to minimize costs Small security-related packet overhead (minimal data expansion due to cryptographic padding and integrity field, for instance) No encumbrance by any existing or pending patents. Sections 4.3.2.1 and 4.3.2.2 briefly describe the CCMP encapsulation and decapsulation procedures. 4.3.2.1 CCMP Encapsulation CCMP encapsulation is the process of generating the cryptographic payload (ciphertext) from the plaintext data. The plaintext data comprises user traffic and a MAC header. The primary steps of CCMP encapsulation are the following: The packet number (PN) maintained for the session is incremented. The PN and other portions of the address field are combined to form the nonce. The identifier for the Temporal Key, or KeyID, and the PN are combined to form the CCMP The frame header is used to construct the Additional Authentication Data (AAD). The AAD is a 22-byte or 28-byte parameter comprising several fields, including several addresses and the quality-of-service control field, that are used as additional input into the CCM authentication The AAD, nonce, and plaintext data are provided as inputs to CCM along with the Temporal Key to encrypt the data. The packet header, the CCM header, and the ciphertext data are concatenated to form the ciphertext (or encapsulated) packet security in the standard model. The design goal was to minimize state on constrained systems CCM is an “authenticate-and-encrypt” block cipher mode of AES. As such, it both encrypts and produces a MIC. As shown, the four inputs to the CCM processing are the following: 128-bit cryptographic key, TK 48-bit nonce (derived for a 48-bit packet number, PN) Additional Authentication Data (AAD) Variable length packet (frame body) with header. CCM uses a new Temporal Key every session—with every new STA-AP association. Unlike TKIP, the use of AES at the core of CCM obviates the need to have per-packet keys. As a result, the two-phase key the CCMP encapsulation.",,
48,draft_0102,train,DevSecOps,Peer Pressure_ Execution-Verified Co-Evolution of Attacker a.txt,ORIJINAL_PDF_KONTROLU_GEREKLI,What is the false-discovery rate of the 7B model inside IRIS when detecting vulnerabilities?,A 7B model inside IRIS reaches a comparable raw count of 52/120 but has a false-discovery rate of 95:4%.,,,Kaynak metin OCR sonrası '95:4%' içeriyor. 95.4% olması muhtemel olsa da özgün grafikten/makaleden onaylanmadan sayısal düzeltme yapılmaz.,"CWE-Bench-Java: CodeQL detects 27/120 (22:5%) and IRIS+GPT-4 55/120 (45:8%) (Li et al., 2025). The same study reports the precision problem directly: a 7B model inside IRIS reaches a comparable raw count (52/120) but at a 95:4% false-discovery rate and Avg F1 of 0:062, below CodeQL’s 0:076, so raw detection without precision does not make a usable detector. Shortcut and degeneracy controls Each vulnerability-introducing diff is paired with a benign diff from the same repository, matched on ﬁle extension and on hunk count within a factor of two, with comment-only and test-only edits excluded from both classes. Two degenerate strategies and two shortcut probes bound how much of the headline number they can explain. always ﬂag / always clear size, hunk and extension probe + control-ﬂow and guard features Balanced accuracy pins either degenerate strategy at exactly 50:0 by construction, whereas on balanced paired data an always-ﬂag classiﬁer attains F1=0:667 while being uninformative. We also report paired accuracy, credit only when both halves of a pair are classiﬁed correctly, which sends both degenerate strategies to zero. The fourth row is the most relevant control for Because a vulnerable scenario is produced by weakening a guard, counts of added and removed conditionals, negations, and guard-name tokens (validate, is_admin, authorize, escape) are a shortcut a diff-level model could exploit without representing security. Given those features the probe reaches 57:9%, so the shortcut is predictive. ceeds the probe by 6:9 points, and by 29:2 on paired accuracy; we do not decompose this margin into shortcut and non-shortcut parts, which would require lesioning these features from the detector’s input. Two properties of the design are intended to suppress the shortcut. Red’s reward on a veriﬁer-certiﬁed clean scenario is 1 ␀ qB, so Red is paid for clean diffs that a reviewer misﬂags, and the gradient pushes clean diffs toward the same guard-touching surface as vulnerabl",,
69,batch500_0089,train,Digital Forensics,Forensic Analysis of the HackRF One with PortaPack Mayhem Fi.txt,MODEL_DUZELTME_ADAYI,What is the format in which forensically relevant data is written to the microSD card of a device running Mayhem v2.0.1?,"Forensically relevant data is written to the microSD card of a device running Mayhem v2.0.1 in a specific format that depends on the real-time clock (RTC) battery state, traces of on-device trimming, and text editing. The exact format and its dependencies are established at the source-code level for forensic analysis.",Which types of deleted or modified digital artifacts were recovered from the HackRF One / PortaPack Mayhem SD card in the described forensic study?,"The study reports recovery of the final part of a deleted IQ capture and a trimmed-away segment of a capture, using the SD-card image and cluster-level recovery.","Kaynak pasajı kesin bir dosya biçimi vermiyor, ancak kurtarılan izleri açıklıyor.","Forensic Analysis of the HackRF One with PortaPack Mayhem Department of Digital Forensics Engineering, Fırat University, Elazığ, Türkiye Software-defined radios (SDRs) have become an inexpensive, portable platform for replay attacks, location spoofing and jamming. The HackRF One can operate stand-alone when fitted with a PortaPack interface board running the open-source Mayhem firmware. No peer-reviewed study has addressed the forensic analysis of this device family. This work establishes, at source-code level, where forensically relevant data reside on the microSD card of a device running Mayhem v2.0.1, in which format, when they are written and how they should be interpreted. The methodology is applied to an SD card image that the author acquired from their own device after four months of real use, and every inference is drawn from the image alone. The workflow covers timestamp rules that depend on the real-time clock (RTC) battery state, traces of on-device trimming and text editing, cluster-level recovery of deleted in-phase/quadrature (IQ) captures, and decoding of recorded signals cross-checked with an independent decoder. In the example image, the final part of a deleted IQ capture and a trimmed-away capture segment are recovered, and six captures are attributed to a single rolling-code on-off keying (OOK) transmitter sharing one 32-bit identifier. The study provides practitioners with an artifact catalogue, interpretation rules and acquisition Keywords: digital forensics; embedded device forensics; FAT32; deleted data recovery; OOK Software-defined radios (SDRs) are devices that use software in place of most of the circuitry that processes radio signals. As a result, a single piece of hardware can work as both a receiver and a transmitter over a wide frequency range. The HackRF One is an open-hardware, lowcost SDR that operates from 1 MHz to 6 GHz in half-duplex mode [1]. In this mode, it either receives or transmits at any one time. When the PortaPack inter",,
86,kaggle1000_0154,val,Incident Response,NIST.SP.800-160v2.txt,MODEL_DUZELTME_ADAYI,What is the time range from initial indication or warning to completion of scans for potentially damaged resources?,The time range from initial indication or warning to completion of scans for potentially damaged resources is not specified in the given information.,Which time-based cyber-resilience metric measures scanning of potentially damaged resources?,The elapsed time from the initial indication or warning until scans for potentially damaged resources are completed.,Sayısal süre aralığı kaynakta yok; verilen metrik tanımı sorulmalı.,"Time from initial indication or warning to completion of scans for potentially damaged resources. • Time from initial indication or warning to completion of component isolation. • Time from initial indication or warning to completion of resource relocation. • Time from initial indication or warning to completion of switch to an alternative. NIST SP 800-160, VOLUME 2 DEVELOPING CYBER RESILIENT SYSTEMS _________________________________________________________________________________________________ • Identify untrustworthy resources and • Restore functionality. • Heighten protections during • Determine the trustworthiness of restored or reconstructed resources. • Time to identify unavailable resources and represent damage in status visualization. • Time between initiation of recovery procedures and completion of documented milestones in the recovery, contingency, or continuity of operations plan. Percentage of cyber resources for which access control is maintained throughout the recovery process. • Percentage of cyber resources for which additional auditing or monitoring is applied during and after the recovery process. Time to bring online a backup network intrusion detection system. Percentage of reconstituted cyber resources which are placed in a restricted enclave for a period after reconstitution. • Percentage of restored or reconstructed (mission-critical, security-critical, supporting) data assets for which data integrity/quality is • Understand adversaries. • Understand dependencies on and among systems containing cyber resources. • Understand the status of resources with respect to threat events • Understand the effectiveness of security controls and controls supporting cyber • Time between receipt of threat intelligence and determination of its relevance. Adversary dwell time in deception • Time since most recent refresh of mission dependency or functional dependency map. Time since last cyber table-top exercise, Red Team exercise, or execution of controlled",,
94,batch1000_0150,train,Malware,malwaredatascience.txt,MODEL_DUZELTME_ADAYI,What does the violin plot in Listing 9-14 reveal about the distribution of file sizes for different malware types?,"The violin plot in Listing 9-14 reveals the distribution of file sizes for different malware types, with the x-axis representing the malware type and the y-axis showing the file size in bytes on a logarithmic scale. This visualization helps identify patterns such as ransomware being associated with larger file sizes compared to other malware types like bitcoin, trojan, and keylogger, which are often detected by fewer scanners.",What do the x-axis and y-axis represent in the malware file-size violin plot described in Listing 9-14?,"The x-axis represents malware type, and the y-axis represents file size in bytes on a base-10 logarithmic scale.",Tarayıcı tespit sayısına ait sonraki grafik ile dosya boyutu grafiği karıştırılmıyor.,"Knowing these patterns could potentially help us to classify unknown files better (a larger file being more likely to be ransomware and less likely to be a worm), or teach us what file sizes we should focus on in a defensive tool targeted at a specific type of malware. Visualizing Malware Trends 173 Making the violin plot takes one plotting call, as shown in Listing 9-14. from matplotlib import pyplot malware = pandas.read_csv(""malware_data.csv"") u axis = seaborn.violinplot(x=malware['type'], y=malware['size']) v axis.set(xlabel=""Malware type"", ylabel=""File size in bytes (log base-10)"", title=""File Sizes by Malware Type"", yscale=""log"") Listing 9-14: Creating a violin plot In Listing 9-14, first we create the violin plot u. Next we tell seaborn to set the axis labels and title and to set the y-axis to log-scale v. Finally, we make the plot appear w. We can also make an analogous plot showing the Number of Detections by Malware Type Number of vendor detections of looking at file size on the y-axis, we’re looking at the number of positives each file received. The results show some interesting trends. For example, ransomware is almost always detected by more than 30 scanners. The bitcoin, trojan, and keylogger malware types, in contrast, are detected by less than 30 scanners a substantial portion of the time, meaning more of these types are slipping past the security industry’s defenses (folks who don’t have the scanners that detect these files installed are likely getting infected by these samples). Listing 9-15 shows how to create the plot shown in Figure 9-10. from matplotlib import pyplot malware = pandas.read_csv(""malware_data.csv"") axis = seaborn.violinplot(x=malware['type'], y=malware['positives']) axis.set(xlabel=""Malware type"", ylabel=""Number of vendor detections"", title=""Number of Detections by Malware Type"") Listing 9-15: Visualizing antivirus detections per malware type The only differences in this code and the previous are that we pass the violinplot functi",,
98,draft_0151,train,Malware,sec_2403.01871v1_Penetration_Testing_of_5G_Core_Netw.txt,MODEL_DUZELTME_ADAYI,"What specific folder was discovered as vulnerable in the Free5gc implementation, leading to the leakage of critical information?","The specific folder discovered as vulnerable in the Free5gc implementation, leading to the leakage of critical information, was /etc/passwd.",Which sensitive operating-system file did the directory-traversal finding on the Free5gc implementation expose?,The /etc/passwd file.,Orijinal kaynak 'folder' diyor ama /etc/passwd bir dosyadır; teknik kavram düzeltildi.,"This isolated scenario turns out to be vulnerable to DDoS attack, exploited using the Hping3 tool in flooding mode, randomizing the source IPs of each request. Although the web server did not crush, this offense may be deemed as effective also considering a large-scale uptake. Directory traversal. For this particular attack, only Free5gc was vulnerable, indeed, the examined folder discovered without knowing its specific path was /etc/passwd enabling in this circumstance the leakage of a large amount of critical information that can be exploited by malicious actors. Clickjacking. About OpenAirInterface, here the attack is not feasible, due to the absence of a web interface. Clickjacking seemed to be a feasible attack, taking into account that either Open5gs and Free5gc enable IFRAME. This was not sufficient, indeed, we did not reach the expected result, mainly due to the presence of the Cross-Origin Resource Sharing policy (CORS) which forestalls to access web resources through different sources than the ones employed by the website. Open5gs turned out to be vulnerable to this attack because it uses a known passphrase. Therefore, once obtained the key, it is possible to change the content of the JWT’s payload and then recreate the proper signature through the previously obtained key, making it valid. Regarding Free5gc, it does not rely on cookies, or hard-coding the credentials, thus it does not save the logged user’s session. Whilst, for the OpenAirInterface, this category of offense is not even applicable, because of the absence of a web interface. Table II presents a summary of the attacks tested against the different 5G core implementations, together with the effectiveness outcome. We notice that not all cores are vulnerable to the same attacks, and for some of them, attacks were not implementable at all. Implementable attacks Database permission leakage Json Web Token robustness TABLE II: Comparison Table between tested attacks over the three mentioned 5G Core. ",,
107,kaggle1000_0727,train,Mobile Security,A Systematic Study of Security Enforcement at Android Inter-.txt,MODEL_DUZELTME_ADAYI,What is the target SDK version requirement for the ServiceStateProvider to perform permission checks?,"The ServiceStateProvider requires a target SDK version to perform permission checks, with a target SDK version gating on permission checks.","According to the cited Android provider study, which target SDK versions let apps bypass ServiceStateProvider permission checks?",Apps targeting SDK versions below 31 (targetSdkVersion < 31) can bypass the permission checks described for ServiceStateProvider.,Kaynak 'targetSdk < 31' diyor; eşik açıkça yazıldı.,"MmsSmsProvider: Write permission declaration (readPermission=READ_SMS but no writePermission; zero-perm app can delete all SMS/MMS) com.android.providers.telephony GoogleSettingsProvider: Read permission declaration (writePermission set but no readPermission; leaks 18 partner settings including privacy prefs) ServiceStateProvider: targetSdkVersion gating on permission checks (targetSdk < 31 bypasses both READ_ GServicesProvider: Permission protection level adequacy (READ_GSERVICES is normal, auto-granted; 1404 config entries including 3 API keys, 86 URLs, 10 certs) com.google.android.gsf BluetoothOppProvider: SQL injection defense (setStrict(true) + path-permission) com.google.android.bluetooth IccProvider, SimPhonebookProvider: Permission ContactsProvider2 zero-perm variant: URI grant scope (ACTION_PICK contact picker grants temporary URI but SQLi from that URI accesses ALL internal tables) com.android.providers.contacts B3: BroadcastReceivers (11 evaluations, all gaps) GmsExternalReceiver (FRP): Per-action caller validation (FrpUpdateIntentOperation writes FRP secret without com.google.android.gms GmsExternalReceiver (CryptAuth): Per-action caller validation (AuthZenEventHandler triggers ForceRegistration of 6 security keys) com.google.android.gms GmsExternalReceiver (systemic): Architectural pattern: single receiver, 31 actions, no android:permission, no per-action validation com.google.android.gms TestingToolsBroadcastReceiver: Build-time stripping of debug code (receiver exported in production; gated by runtime Phenotype flag, not build-time removal) 11 Google apps (Gmail, Dialer, CalendarDebugReceiver: Export control on debug receiver (accepts SECRET_CODE broadcast) com.android.providers.calendar TestReceiver: Export control on test receiver (no permission, no build-type guard; forwards SIMULATE_ STATE to privileged StateService) com.google.android.apps.scone MediaOutputDialogReceiver: Protected broadcast NOT declared as protected; sibling LAUNCH_SYSTEM_ packa",,
116,batch500_0260,val,Mobile Security,Penetrating the Reality Wall_ Long-Range and Through-Wall AR.txt,MODEL_DUZELTME_ADAYI,What is the maximum distance the attacker can be from the victim's VR device to still be considered within the threat model?,The maximum distance the attacker can be from the victim's VR device to still be considered within the threat model is 8.0 meters.,What physical positioning does the wireless side-channel threat model assume for the attacker targeting an AR/VR user?,"The attacker can position a wireless-power sniffer in an adjacent room and observe Wi-Fi signals from the target router. The study gives approximately 5.0–8.0 m as an example of a practical nearby range, not a guaranteed maximum.",8.0 m kesin üst sınır değil; örnek yakınlık aralığı olarak tanımlanıyor.,"Attacker’s Capabilities. Unlike previous AR/VR attacks, we do not assume that the attacker can compromise the VR headset by installing malware to obtain motion sensor data or monitor changes in system workload [4, 23, 24, 50, 64, 68, 70–72]. Next, the attacker has no Line-of-Sight (LoS) view of VR users and cannot place cameras or hand trackers to record videos of the victim’s head movements and hand gestures directly [10, 15, 30, 35], nor can a suspicious device be placed in close proximity (e.g., less than 2.0 m) to record button-clicking sounds or emitted infrared signals from VR controllers [25, 37]. Moreover, we do not assume that the victim would join unauthorized shared virtual spaces [28, 62] or multi-user VR apps [55] to share avatar data publicly (e.g., hand movements, gaze information). Furthermore, the victim can use the VR device while (a) Before interference removal. (b) After interference removal ence caused by other non-target connected devices. sitting on the stationary boundary or moving in the playing zone, without the physical restrictions of a fixed position required in other side-channel attacks [1, 27]. Note that we only assume that the attacker can place the wireless power sniffer in the adjacent room to capture the Wi-Fi signals from the target router and use the measurable wireless power patterns to infer multi-level and fine-grained privacy from the AR/VR users. Following the research line of prior wireless side-channel studies [1, 27, 38], our threat model inherently requires the attacker to be physically present in a space adjacent to the victim within a limited yet practical proximity (e.g., 5.0 m–8.0 m). This scenario is naturally satisfied in apartments, hotels, and shared offices without requiring same-room device placement or malware installation, although the attack’s effectiveness depends on the propagation characteristics of the wireless medium and is influenced by intervening obstacles and co-channel devices in the environment. ",,
124,kaggle1000_0523,train,Network Security,Computer Security Division 2010 Annual Report.txt,MODEL_DUZELTME_ADAYI,"What specific IETF RFC documents were added to the revised version of SP 800-81, Secure Domain Name System (DNS) Deployment Guide, published in April 2010?","The specific IETF RFC documents added to the revised version of SP 800-81, Secure Domain Name System (DNS) Deployment Guide, published in April 2010 include:

- DNNSEC Operational Practices (RFC 4641)
- Automated Updates for DNS Security (DNSSEC) Trust Anchors (RFC 5011)
- DNSSecurity (DNSSEC) Hashed Authenticated Denial of Existence (RFC 5155)
- HMAC SHA TSIG Algorithm Identifiers (RFC 463",Which IETF RFC numbers does the cited NIST annual report list among the DNSSEC-related updates to SP 800-81 Revision 1?,"RFC 4641, RFC 5011, RFC 5155, and RFC 4635.",OCR bozuk RFC adları yerine kaynakta ayırt edilebilen numaralar kullanıldı.,"To counter these threats, the Internet Engineering Task Force (IETF) developed a set of specifications for securing DNS called DNS Security Extensions (DNSSEC) to provide origin au­ thentication and data integrity for all responses from the DNS. In partnership with the Department of Homeland Security, NIST has been actively involved in promoting the deployment of DNSSEC Computer Security Division Annual Report – 2010 The significant achievements in FY2010 are as follows: Publication of the revised version of SP 800-81 Revision 1, Secure Domain Name System (DNS) Deployment Guide, in April 2010, after two rounds of public comments. The major additions/changes in this revision are the following: Updated recommendations for all cryptographic opera­ tions relating to digital signing of DNS records, verifica­ tion of the signatures, Zone Transfer, Dynamic Updates, Key Management and Authenticated Denial of Exis­ The addition of IETF RFC documents that have formed the basis for the updated recommendations including: DNNSEC Operational Practices (RFC 4641), Automated Updates for DNS Security (DNSSEC) Trust Anchors (RFC 5011),DNSSecurity(DNSSEC)HashedAuthenticatedDe­ nial of Existence (RFC 5155) and HMAC SHA TSIG Algo­ rithm Identifiers (RFC 4635). Additional FIPS standards and NIST guidelines incorpo­ rated into the updated recommendations including: FIPS 198-1, Keyed-Hash Message Authentication Code (HMAC), FIPS 186-3, Digital Signature Standard, and SP 800-57 Part 1 and Part 3, Recommendations for Key using DNS Software offering NSD, in addition to Berkeley Internet Name Domain (BIND); Guidelines on procedures for migrating to a new Cryptographic Algorithm for signing of the Zone Guidelines for procedures for migrating to Next Se­ cure 3 (NSEC3) specifications from NSEC for providing authenticated denial of existence (Section 11.6); Deployment Guidelines for Split-Zone under different scenarios (Section 11.7). Assisting GSA in deploying DNSSEC on the .gov Top Level Domain",,
135,kaggle1000_0065,test,Network Security,CyberHull_ Deterministic Malicious URL Isolation via Convex.txt,MODEL_DUZELTME_ADAYI,What type of dataset was used to empirically test the CyberHull algorithm?,A benchmark dataset was used to test the CyberHull algorithm.,What kind of cybersecurity traffic does the CyberHull approach aim to distinguish using convex-hull-based geometry?,"It aims to distinguish malicious from benign web traffic, including phishing-related traffic patterns, by using deterministic geometric boundaries rather than relying solely on probabilistic models.",Kaynak veri setinin somut adını vermiyor; çalışmanın açık problemi soruluyor.,"Popular machine learning techniques such as Principal Component Analysis (PCA) assume very strong assumptions on linear separability and normally distributed feature spaces [8]. However, modern phishing ecosystems exhibit strongly non-linear characteristics with irregular entropy distributions [3], semantic anomalies and dynamically evolving hyperlink relationships [9, 10]. Thus, simple linear transformation methods often do not yield reliable decision boundaries for complex adversarial web traffic patterns. This practical problem in using linear classifiers, exacerbated by the ""black box"" nature of deep neural networks [15, 7, 16], has resulted in an important operational gap in the field of threat identification in real time. For applications in which predictability and transparency are key, scientists studying defensive mechanisms in physical spaces like autonomous UAV swarm tracking, missile trajectory representation, and radar border mapping have effectively eliminated probabilistic guesswork by employing computational geometry [13]. In such high-stakes areas of study, N-dimensional Convex Hulls form inflexible and unforgiving spatial borders surrounding known objects. although these robust mathematical concepts have found widespread use in the analysis of physical battlefields, they have seen little application in cybersecurity to date. Consequently, one major limitation is that no deterministic and geometrically meaningful cybersecurity approach has been developed yet, which can effectively separate malicious web traffic without depending solely on probabilistic learning models. CyberHull is designed precisely for this purpose. This paper solves the problem by applying topological principles to map non-linear traits of the cyber domain into individual geometric Vaults [6]. In this section, we begin by describing the seed dataset utilized in this work. Benchmark Dataset Description In order to test the CyberHull algorithm empirically, we used a data set based ",,
165,batch1000_0726,test,System Security,Extending Layered Attestation into ARM Application Run-Times.txt,MODEL_DUZELTME_ADAYI,What are the primary functions of a Root of Trust for Measurement (RTM)?,"RTM primarily enables secure boot, cryptographic key management, and platform remote integrity verification. It focuses on system integrity by securely measuring software, including load-time code integrity and run-time integrity, such as control and data flow.",What is the primary role of a Root of Trust for Measurement (RTM)?,"An RTM securely measures software integrity, such as code integrity at load time and execution-time properties including control and data flow. Secure key storage is instead a role associated with a Root of Trust for Storage (RTS).",RTM ile RTS işlevleri ayrıldı.,"In modern systems, RoTs enable functions such as secure boot, cryptographic key management, and platform remote integrity verification (a.k.a., remote attestation). RoTs are generally categorized as RoTs for measurement and A Root of Trust for Measurement (RTM) focuses on system integrity by securely measuring software (either statically, i.e., at load-time or run-time/execution properties). Examples of measured properties include load-time code integrity and run-time integrity (e.g., control and data flow). A Root of Trust for Storage (RTS) provides secure protection for sensitive cryptographic material and other security-critical data. Unlike an RTM, which focuses on integrity measurement, the RTS ensures the confidentiality and controlled usage of secrets such as encryption keys, certificates, authentication credentials, and attestation keys. In many architectures, cryptographic operations involving protected keys are performed entirely within the RTS boundary, preventing key material from ever being exposed in plaintext to untrusted software. ARA establishes a run-time RoT within a user-space process. Section 7 presents two case studies demonstrating how ARA can be instantiated as RTM and RTS for user-space Layered Attestation and Application Integrity Modern mobile platforms (such as iOS and Android) provide hardware-backed RTMs for attestation services that allow application servers to verify the integrity of both application code and aspects of the underlying execution environment 1Some of the literature distinguishes a third category, namely RoT for Reporting (RTR), used when the RTM result is to be signed to generate an externally verifiable report that can be checked by a remote verifier, e.g., in remote attestation protocols. In this paper, we deliberately couple RTR as a sub-function of RTM. sions for domains based on their IDs at or near load-time. Two prominent examples are Apple’s App Attest [2] and Google’s Play Integrity API [24]. These systems aim ",,
170,batch_new500_0281,train,Web Security,OWASP_Application_Security_Verification_Standard_5.0.0_en (1).txt,MODEL_DUZELTME_ADAYI,What method should be used for HTTP requests to sensitive functionality to defend against browser-based request forgery attacks?,"HTTP requests to sensitive functionality should use appropriate HTTP methods such as POST, PUT, PATCH, or DELETE, and not methods defined by the HTTP specification as ""safe"" such as HEAD, OPTIONS, or GET.","Which HTTP methods does OWASP ASVS recommend for sensitive functionality, and what additional checks help defend against CSRF?","Sensitive state-changing operations should use suitable methods such as POST, PUT, PATCH, or DELETE instead of safe methods such as GET or HEAD. CSRF defenses additionally include validating anti-forgery tokens, appropriate non-safelisted headers, or robust origin/preflight validation; choosing a method alone is not sufficient.",OWASP pasajındaki iki farklı gereksinim doğru bağlamda birleştirildi.,"This mechanism will be critical for endpoints designed to be called from a different origin, but it can also be a useful request forgery prevention mechanism for endpoints which are not designed to be called from a different origin. Verify that, if the application does not rely on the CORS preflight mechanism to prevent disallowed cross‑origin requests to use sensitive functionality, these requests are validated to ensure they originate from the application itself. This may be done by using and validating anti‑forgery tokens or requiring extra HTTP header fields that are not CORS‑safelisted request‑header fields. This is to defend against browser‑based request forgery attacks, commonly known as cross‑site request forgery (CSRF). Verify that, if the application relies on the CORS preflight mechanism to prevent disallowed cross‑origin use of sensitive functionality, it is not possible to call the functionality with a request which does not trigger a CORS‑preflight request. This may require checking the values of the ‘Origin’ and ‘Content‑Type’request header fields or using an extra header field that is not a CORS‑safelisted header‑field. Application Security Verification Standard Verify that HTTP requests to sensitive functionality use appropriate HTTP methods such as POST, PUT, PATCH, or DELETE, and not methods defined by the HTTP specification as “safe”such as HEAD, OPTIONS, or GET. Alternatively, strict validation of the Sec‑Fetch‑* request header fields can be used to ensure that the request did not originate from an inappropriate cross‑origin call, a navigation request, or a resource load (such as an image source) where this is not expected. Verify that separate applications are hosted on different hostnames to leverage the restrictions provided by same‑origin policy, including how documents or scripts loaded by one origin can interact with resources from another origin and hostname‑based restrictions on cookies. Verify that messages received by the postMessage i",,
